#!perl

=head1 NAME

nergal - A mojolicious script for handling submissions of files for detonation.

=head1 SYNOPSIS

sudo -u cape hypnotoad -f /usr/local/bin/nergal

sudo -u cape nergal daemon -m production -l 'http://*:8080'

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This script is meant for running locally on a CAPEv2. It allows remote machines to
to submit files for detonation.

To work, this script needs to be running as the same user as CAPEv2.

This will write activity to syslog.

A systemd service file is provided at 'systemd/nergal.service' in this modules
tarball. It runs nergal under L<hypnotoad>, Mojolicious' preforking server, as the
user and group 'cape' via the unit's 'User=' and 'Group=' directives, so edit
those if you need a different user. It expects the enviromental
'/usr/local/etc/nergal.env' file to be setup with the variable 'LISTEN_ON'. So lets
say you want to listen on http://192.168.14.15:8080, it would be like below.

    LISTEN_ON="http://192.168.14.15:8080"

More than one may be listened on, seperated by whitespace, which the single
'daemon -l' this replaced could not do.

    LISTEN_ON="http://192.168.14.15:8080 http://127.0.0.1:8080"

For TLS, make it a 'https://' listen and point 'cert' and 'key' at the
certificate and its key via the query string, as below.

    LISTEN_ON="https://192.168.14.15:8443?cert=/usr/local/etc/nergal/cert.pem&key=/usr/local/etc/nergal/key.pem"

The '&' does not need escaping, as systemd runs 'ExecStart' directly rather than
through a shell. Both files must be readable by the user nergal runs as, 'cape'
by default, which for the key generally means giving it to that user with mode
0400 rather than leaving it where it landed.

A 'https://' listen with no 'cert' and 'key' still comes up, but on Mojolicious'
built in test certificate, which is self signed, issued to 'localhost', and
shipped with its private key in the Mojolicious tarball, so it secures nothing
against anyone who bothers to look. Always set the two of them.

'cert' should be the full chain where a intermediate is involved, as nothing else
is sent to the client, and a submitter validating the certificate fails without
it. Where the certificate is a private one, the submitter has to be told to trust
the CA that signed it.

    LISTEN_ON="https://192.168.14.15:8443?cert=/usr/local/etc/nergal/fullchain.pem&key=/usr/local/etc/nergal/key.pem&ca=/usr/local/etc/nergal/ca.pem"

Renewing a certificate means restarting nergal, as it is read at listen time. The
rest of what may be set here, such as 'ciphers' and 'version', is covered in
L<Mojo::Server::Daemon/listen>.

Hypnotoad takes its settings from the application rather than the command line,
so nergal hands it the ones in "ENVIROMENTAL VARIABLES" below, 'LISTEN_ON' being
the one that matters. With none of them set it comes up on its own default of
http://*:8080 with four workers, which is unlikely to be what is wanted.

Submissions are handled by a pool of workers, so unlike the single process
'daemon' this replaced, more than one may be in flight at a time. A worker is
busy for as long as the sample takes to hand off to CAPE, so 'NERGAL_WORKERS' is
effectively how many submissions may be dealt with at once. The tracking int in
the log is per worker rather than global, so pair it with the pid syslog records
when following a submission through the log.

Deploy with a plain 'systemctl restart nergal'. Hypnotoad's hot deployment, so
running it a second time or signalling it with a USR2, replaces the manager
process with a new one, which systemd sees as the service having died and takes
it from there, so it is not something to do under the unit.

Alternatively, this script can be invoked as a CGI script if it is ran as the user CAPEv2 is.

=head1 CONFIGURATION

Everything is read from the cape_utils INI, '/usr/local/etc/cape_utils.ini' by
default, out of its root section. So if cape_utils has been configured and is
working, this just needs 'incoming' pointed somewhere usable.

The keys below are the ones specific to nergal. The rest of the INI is what it
always is, with how a submission is actually detonated coming from the usual
cape_utils settings, 'base', 'cape_runas', 'timeout', and the like.

    - incoming :: Directory incoming files are placed in for submission. The
      submission JSON, the checksum store, the name and task links, and the tmp
      dir a submission is staged in are all subdirectories beneath it. Must be
      writable by the user nergal runs as. See "INCOMING DIR STRUCTURE" below
      for the layout.
      - Default :: /malware/client-incoming

    - auth :: How a submitter is authed. 'ip' is by subnet only, 'apikey' by key
      only, 'both' requires the two of them, and 'either' takes one or the other.
      See "SECURITY" below.
      - Default :: ip

    - subnets :: Comma seperated list of subnets, v4 or v6, submissions are
      accepted from. Only used where 'auth' is 'ip', 'both', or 'either'.
      - Default :: 192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8

    - apikey :: The key a submitter must send as the 'apikey' param. An empty
      value never matches, so key based auth is off until it is set.
      - Default :: ''

    - results_auth :: As 'auth', but for results fetching, GET /results/*, so
      that may be handed out seperately from the ability to submit. See
      "RESULTS FETCHING" below.
      - Default :: ip

    - results_subnets :: As 'subnets', but for results fetching.
      - Default :: 192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8

    - results_apikey :: As 'apikey', but for results fetching.
      - Default :: ''

    - submission_gate :: Command ran per submission to decide what becomes of it,
      with the verdict taken from how it exits. Empty disables it. See
      "SUBMISSION GATE" below.
      - Default :: ''

    - submission_gate_timeout :: How many seconds the gate is given to run before
      it is killed and the submission dropped as an error.
      - Default :: 30

    - enable_access_logs :: If HTTP access logs should be written under the
      'http_access_logs' directory beneath 'incoming', one file per day.
      Requires the optional module L<Mojolicious::Plugin::AccessLog>. See
      "HTTP ACCESS LOGS" below.
      - Default :: 0

=head1 SECURITY

By default this will auth of the remote IP via the setting 'subnets', which by default is
'192.168.0.0/16,127.0.0.1/8,::1/128,172.16.0.0/12,10.0.0.0/8'. This value is a comma seperated
string of subnets to accept submissions from.

To enable the use of a API key, set the value of 'apikey' and set 'auth' to 'apikey'
(key only), 'both' (key and IP), or 'either' (key or IP).

=head1 SUBMISSION GATE

The 'submission_gate' setting is a command ran per submission to decide what
becomes of it. It is empty by default, which disables it. What is done with the
submission is taken from how it exits.

    - 0 :: Accept it. Saved and submitted as normal.

    - 1 :: Accept and save it, but do not submit it.

    - 2 :: Accept it, but neither save nor submit it. Nothing is kept.

    - 3 :: Deny it, temporarily. Replied to with a 444.

    - 4 :: Deny it, permanently. Replied to with a 445.

    - 5 :: Deny it, generically, for anything else. Replied to with a 403.

Anything else is a gate that is broken rather than one making a call, and is
handled like any other error, meaning the submission is dropped and replied to
with a 400. A gate that could not be ran, timed out, or was killed is treated the
same. So a broken gate never quietly accepts or denies anything, but a bad
'submission_gate' does stop submissions until it is fixed.

The gate is described the submission via 'NERGAL_' prefixed enviromental
variables, which include the file itself, the submitted JSON, the slug, the
checksums, the mime, and the flow info. Its stdout, stderr, and exit are all
written to syslog, and where the submission is saved they are recorded in the
incoming JSON as well. How long it is given to run is set via
'submission_gate_timeout', which defaults to 30 seconds.

See L<CAPE::Utils::Nergal/"SUBMISSION GATE"> for the full details.

=head1 HTTP ACCESS LOGS

Where 'enable_access_logs' is set, every request is written in combinedio
format, Apache combined plus bytes received and sent, via
L<Mojolicious::Plugin::AccessLog> to a daily file under the
'incoming' directory, 'http_access_logs/access-<YYYY-MM-DD>.log'. That module
is optional and only needed where this is enabled.

The day comes from local time and the log rolls over to a new file with it.

The 'http_access_logs' directory is created at start up where missing and the
day's log is opened then as well, so an unwritable dir is caught at start up
rather than on the first request. Should any of that fail, be it the module
not being installed, the incoming directory being absent, or the directory or
log not being creatable, it is logged and nergal runs on without access
logging until it is restarted. A rollover that fails is logged and retried per
request, with requests logged to the old day's file in the meantime.

=head1 SUBMISSION

Submissions must be made using the post method, to any path. The path is not
matched on at all, so C<< / >>, C<< /submit >>, or a CGI one like
C<< /cgi-bin/bluedot-submit.cgi >> all land in the same place, and a submitter
already pointed at some other path keeps working as is.

A GET of a submission URL, which is what pasting it into a browser to see if
nergal is up does, is replied to with a 405 and an C<Allow: POST>. That is the
method being wrong rather than the URL, so it is not a 404. To actually check a
submission path end to end, POST the ten byte ping described in L</PINGING>.
GET is only used for results fetching, see L</"RESULTS FETCHING">.

=head2 Submission Parameters

Required ones are as below.

    - filename :: The file being submitted.

The following are optional and more or less "free form",
but helps to set them to something sane and relevant.

    - type :: The type of submission. Generally going
              to be 'manual' or 'suricata_extract'.

    - json :: Metadata for the submission. For a submission
              coming from Suricata this is the EVE fileinfo
              record with a 'suricata_extract_submit' section
              added to it.

=head2 Submission Name Format

The 'json' param is optional. Where it is not sent, nergal will recover the flow
info and slug from the submitted filename provided it is in the standard format
below, which is what suricata_extract_submit builds its names in.

    <src_ip>-<src_port>-<dest_ip>-<dest_port>-<proto>-<sha1>-<slug>-<epoch>-<mime>

    - src_ip / dest_ip :: A valid IPv4 or IPv6 address.

    - src_port / dest_port :: An integer from 0 to 65535.

    - proto :: 'TCP' or 'UDP'. Case does not matter.

    - sha1 :: Hex digest of the file as of prior to sending, in upper, lower,
              or mixed case, and of any length. This is what the received file
              is checked against to detect a file changing in transit. Current
              submitters put a full sha1 here, but older ones put the first 18
              characters of the md5 instead and nothing in the name marks which
              it is, so it is recorded as '.suricata_extract_submit.md5' and
              nergal_extend compares it as a prefix against both the sha1 and
              the md5 taken on receipt.

    - slug :: The group/client/whathaveya the submitter belongs to. Must not be
              all digits. Stats are broken out per slug by nergal_extend.

    - epoch :: UTC unix time the file was extracted at, as a positive integer.

    - mime :: The mime type with any '/' replaced by '_', eg
              'application_x-dosexec'. Must therefore contain a '_'.

The mime is the only field that may contain a '-'. Nothing else in the name
may, the slug included, as the name is split on '-' with a limit of 9 and
everything past the ninth field is taken as part of the mime.

Matching is all or nothing. Unless all nine fields are present and valid the
name is left alone and nothing is filled in, so a manually submitted file keeps
its own name and simply has no flow info or slug. A slug containing a '-'
shifts every field after it and fails validation, so it is declined rather than
mis-parsed.

The name is only ever fallen back on where no usable 'json' was sent. A
submitter that sent a body is taken at its word about what the submission is and
has nothing filled in from the name, even the fields it left out. A body that
does not decode, or that decodes to something other than a hash, counts as none
being sent.

Only the file bit of the submitted name is ever used. A name containing path
separators is reduced to its last component, and one with nothing usable left
after that is rejected.

See L<CAPE::Utils::Nergal/parse_name>.

=head1 RESULTS FETCHING

Detonation results written by CAPEv2 under C<< <base>/storage/analyses/<task_id>/ >>
can be fetched via GET.

    GET /results/<task_id>            -- JSON array of available result files
    GET /results/<task_id>/<path>     -- fetch one result file

Only a fixed set of files may be fetched: C<reports/lite.json>,
C<reports/report.json>, C<reports/report.html>, C<reports/summary-report.html>,
and C<shots/*.jpg>. Anything else, including path traversal attempts, returns a
404.

This is gated separately from submission via the C<results_auth>,
C<results_apikey>, and C<results_subnets> config values. See
L<CAPE::Utils/check_remote_results>. The API key, if used, is passed as the
C<apikey> query parameter.

=head1 PINGING

If you submit a file of size 10 matching /1234567890/, so the ten bytes
"1234567890" and nothing else, it will return "TEST RECIEVED\n", provided the
submitter is authed. At that point it stops processing it, so nothing is saved
or submitted to CAPE.

=head1 ENVIROMENTAL VARIABLES

The three hypnotoad ones, 'LISTEN_ON', 'NERGAL_PID_FILE', and 'NERGAL_WORKERS',
are only looked at when nergal is ran under L<hypnotoad>, which is what the
systemd unit does. Nothing else pays them any mind, so a 'daemon' invocation
still takes its listen from C<< -l >>.

=head2 LISTEN_ON

What to listen on, one or more Mojolicious listen URLs seperated by whitespace.
See L<Mojo::Server::Daemon/listen> for the full syntax, and "DESCRIPTION" above
for the TLS ones.

If undef, hypnotoad's own default of 'http://*:8080' is used.

=head2 MOJO_MODE

The Mojolicious mode, which nergal defaults to 'production' rather than
Mojolicious' own default of 'development'. Development mode replies to a request
it has no route for with a page listing every route, the request internals, and
the module versions, which is not something a submitter should be shown.

This is only a default, so setting it, or 'PLACK_ENV', still works, as does
C<< -m >> on the command line. It matters most for CGI, where there is no command
line to pass C<< -m >> on. Hypnotoad defaults to production as well, so under the
systemd unit this only comes up if something has set it to development.

=head2 NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE

This is the max request size, which will affect the size of files the can be submitted.

If undef, this defaults to 1G, 1073741824.

=head2 NERGAL_PID_FILE

Where hypnotoad keeps its process id file. The systemd unit points this at
'/run/nergal/nergal.pid', a runtime dir it has systemd create as the user nergal
runs as.

If undef, hypnotoad's own default is used, which is a 'hypnotoad.pid' sitting
next to the nergal script, meaning in a bin dir that the user it runs as should
not be able to write to. So set it to somewhere that user owns.

=head2 NERGAL_WORKERS

How many hypnotoad worker processes to run, as a positive integer. Anything else
is logged and ignored. A worker is tied up for as long as handing the sample to
CAPE takes, so this is how many submissions may be handled at once.

If undef, hypnotoad's own default of 4 is used.

=head1 INCOMING DIR STRUCTURE

The following directories are used under the incoming directory.

    sha256
    json
    name_to_sha256
    task_to_json
    tmp

Any of them that are missing are created at start up, so only the incoming
directory itself has to be made beforehand. That one is never created for you,
as it is normally a mount and creating it would turn a failed mount into samples
landing on the wrong filesystem. Where it is missing, that is logged and every
submission is refused until it turns up, at which point nergal picks up again
without needing a restart.

"tmp" is where the file is written to first to get the checksum.

"sha256" is where it is moved to after we have the checksum if it does not
already exist.

"json" is where the incoming JSON is written to based on filename.

"name_to_sha256" contains links from incoming filename to the sha256
in question. If it already exists it is updated to the newest one.

"task_to_json" contains links to from the task name to the JSON it is for.

Where 'enable_access_logs' is set, "http_access_logs" is also created here and
is where the daily HTTP access logs are written. See "HTTP ACCESS LOGS" above.

=cut

use strict;
use warnings;

# Mojolicious defaults to development mode, where anything it has no route for is
# replied to with a page listing every route, the request internals, and the
# module versions. Under 'daemon' the mode is set via '-m production', as the
# systemd unit does, but a CGI invocation has no command line to put that on and
# so gets the debug page, which is both noise and more than a submitter should be
# told. So default it here instead.
#
# Has to be a BEGIN as Mojolicious::Lite builds the app when it is imported. Only
# defaulted, never overridden, so 'MOJO_MODE'/'PLACK_ENV' still work, as does
# '-m', which is parsed after this and wins as the mode is looked up lazily.
BEGIN {
	if ( !defined( $ENV{'MOJO_MODE'} ) && !defined( $ENV{'PLACK_ENV'} ) ) {
		$ENV{'MOJO_MODE'} = 'production';
	}
}

use Mojolicious::Lite -signatures;
use CAPE::Utils          ();
use CAPE::Utils::Nergal  ();
use CAPE::Utils::LogDrek qw( log_drek );
use POSIX                qw( strftime );
use IO::Handle           ();

# one instance shared across requests so the tracking int keeps incrementing
my $submitter = CAPE::Utils::Nergal->new;

# Make the incoming sub dirs up front so a fresh install works without them being
# created by hand, which otherwise 400s every submission until someone notices.
#
# Not fatal, as the incoming dir may be a mount that is not up yet and receive
# checks per submission anyway, so this recovers on its own once it appears. It
# is logged to both syslog and STDERR so it still shows in the journal.
eval { $submitter->make_dirs; };
if ($@) {
	my $error_message = 'incoming dir setup failed, submissions will be refused until it is sorted... ' . $@;
	log_drek( 'err', $error_message, undef, 'nergal' );
	warn($error_message);
}

# HTTP access logging is optional and off by default via enable_access_logs.
# Where enabled the http_access_logs dir is made under the incoming dir and
# Mojolicious::Plugin::AccessLog, an optional module, is handed a logger writing
# beneath it, one file per day.
#
# Not fatal, as being unable to log accesses is not worth refusing submissions
# over. It is logged to both syslog and STDERR so it still shows in the journal,
# and nergal runs on without access logging until restarted.
my $access_log_dir;
my $access_log_fh;
my $access_log_day = '';

# Opens the daily access log for the passed day and swaps it in, which is what
# rotates the log. Mojolicious::Plugin::AccessLog opens a path handed to it once
# and holds it forever, so it is handed a coderef writing via this instead.
#
# Args:
#     - day :: The day to open the log for, as a 'YYYY-MM-DD' string from local
#       time. Becomes part of the file name, 'access-<day>.log' under
#       $access_log_dir.
#
# Returns nothing. On success $access_log_fh holds the new handle, autoflushed
# so lines are not sat in a buffer, $access_log_day holds the day, and any
# previous handle has been closed. Dies where the file could not be opened,
# changing neither.
#
#     $open_access_log->( strftime( '%Y-%m-%d', localtime ) );
my $open_access_log = sub {
	my ($day) = @_;

	my $access_log_file = $access_log_dir . '/access-' . $day . '.log';
	open( my $new_access_log_fh, '>>', $access_log_file )
		or die 'access log, "' . $access_log_file . '", could not be opened... ' . $!;
	$new_access_log_fh->autoflush(1);

	if ( defined($access_log_fh) ) {
		close($access_log_fh);
	}
	$access_log_fh  = $new_access_log_fh;
	$access_log_day = $day;

	return;
}; ## end $open_access_log = sub

eval {
	my $cape_util = CAPE::Utils->new;
	if ( $cape_util->{config}->{_}->{enable_access_logs} ) {
		my $incoming = $cape_util->{config}->{_}->{incoming};
		$access_log_dir = $incoming . '/http_access_logs';
		# mkdir rather than make_path, as the incoming dir is deliberately never
		# created... it is normally a mount and make_path would quietly make it,
		# putting the access log on the wrong filesystem
		if ( !-d $incoming ) {
			die 'incoming directory, "' . $incoming . '", does not exist';
		}
		if ( !-d $access_log_dir && !mkdir($access_log_dir) ) {
			die 'access log directory, "' . $access_log_dir . '", could not be created... ' . $!;
		}

		# opened up front so an unwritable dir is caught here, where failing
		# disables access logging cleanly, rather than on the first request
		$open_access_log->( strftime( '%Y-%m-%d', localtime ) );

		plugin 'AccessLog' => {
			format => 'combinedio',
			log    => sub {
				my $day = strftime( '%Y-%m-%d', localtime );
				if ( $day ne $access_log_day ) {
					# a failed rollover is logged and retried per request, with
					# lines going to the old day's file rather than being lost
					eval { $open_access_log->($day); };
					if ($@) {
						log_drek( 'err', 'access log rollover failed... ' . $@, undef, 'nergal' );
					}
				}
				print {$access_log_fh} $_[0];
				return;
			},
		};
	} ## end if ( $cape_util->{config}->{_}->{enable_access_logs...})
};
if ($@) {
	my $error_message = 'access log setup failed, continuing without it... ' . $@;
	log_drek( 'err', $error_message, undef, 'nergal' );
	warn($error_message);
}

# results fetching. list what result files exist for a task...
get '/results/:task_id' => sub ($c) {
	my $result = $submitter->results_list(
		task_id   => $c->stash('task_id'),
		remote_ip => $c->tx->original_remote_address,
		apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
	);
	$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status} );
	return;
};

# ...and fetch a single one of them. '*subpath' captures the slashes in eg
# 'reports/lite.json' or 'shots/0001.jpg'. 'path' can't be used as the
# placeholder name as it is a reserved Mojolicious stash value.
get '/results/:task_id/*subpath' => sub ($c) {
	my $result;
	eval {
		$result = $submitter->results_fetch(
			task_id   => $c->stash('task_id'),
			path      => $c->stash('subpath'),
			remote_ip => $c->tx->original_remote_address,
			apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
		);
	};
	if ($@) {
		log_drek( 'err', $@ );
		$c->render( text => "Error... please see syslog\n", status => 400 );
		return;
	}

	if ( $result->{path} ) {
		$c->res->headers->content_type( $result->{content_type} );
		$c->reply->file( $result->{path} );
	} else {
		$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status} );
	}
	return;
}; ## end sub

# Submissions are POST only, so a GET of the submission URL, which is what
# happens when someone pastes it into a browser to see if nergal is up, matches
# nothing and falls through to the not found handler. That is a 404 that reads
# like the URL is wrong when the method is the only thing that was, so answer it
# with a 405 naming the method that is wanted instead.
#
# Declared after the results routes so those still win, and as with the POST
# routes below, both '/*' and '/' are needed as the two do not overlap.
sub method_not_allowed {
	my $c = $_[0];

	$c->res->headers->allow('POST');
	$c->render( text => "nergal: submissions are POST only, see the nergal docs\n", status => 405, );

	return;
}
get '/*' => sub ($c) {
	method_not_allowed($c);
};
get '/' => sub ($c) {
	method_not_allowed($c);
};

# two are needed as /* and / don't overlap... so pass it to the_stuff to actually handle it
post '/*' => sub ($c) {
	the_stuff($c);
};
post '/' => sub ($c) {
	the_stuff($c);
};

# Handles a submission, adapting between Mojolicious and the backend by pulling
# the pieces of the request off the controller, handing them to
# CAPE::Utils::Nergal->receive, and rendering whatever comes back.
#
# It exists because the two routes it backs, '/*' and '/', do not overlap in
# Mojolicious and so both have to be declared, and neither wants its own copy of
# this.
#
# The failure detail goes to syslog rather than into the response, as a submitter
# that has just tripped over something has no need to be told the internals of
# it.
#
# Args:
#     - c :: The Mojolicious::Controller for the request. The remote address,
#       the apikey and json parameters, the uploaded file, and whether the
#       request ran past the max request size are taken off it. Required.
#
# Returns nothing, the point of it being the response it renders. That is what
# receive() decided on, or a 400 when receive() died.
#
#     post '/*' => sub ($c) {
#         the_stuff($c);
#     };
sub the_stuff {
	my $c = $_[0];

	my $result;
	eval {
		$result = $submitter->receive(
			remote_ip => $c->{tx}{original_remote_address},
			apikey    => $c->param('apikey'),
			raw_json  => $c->param('json'),
			upload    => $c->param('filename'),
			oversized => $c->req->is_limit_exceeded,
		);
	};
	if ($@) {
		log_drek( 'err', $@ );
		$c->render( text => "Error... please see syslog\n", status => 400, );
		return;
	}

	$c->render( text => $result->{body}, status => $result->{status}, );

	return;
} ## end sub the_stuff

# ensure we have something set for $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} and that it is a
# int
if ( defined( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} ) ) {
	if ( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} !~ /^\d\d*$/ ) {
		log_drek(
			'err',
			'$ENV{NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE} set to "'
				. $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'}
				. '" which does not match /^\d\d*$/ ... defaulting to 1073741824',
		);
		$ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} = 1073741824;
	}
} else {
	$ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} = 1073741824;
}
app->max_request_size( $ENV{'NERGAL_MAX_REQ_SIZE'} );

# Hypnotoad, which is what the systemd unit runs nergal under, takes its settings
# from the application config rather than the command line, so the enviroment is
# mapped into that here. Without this 'LISTEN_ON' would be quietly ignored and
# every install would come up on hypnotoad's own default of http://*:8080.
#
# Only what is actually set is put in there, as anything present in the config
# overrides hypnotoad's default even when it is undef. None of it affects the
# 'daemon' command, which takes its listen from '-l' and does not look at this.
my %hypnotoad;

# More than one may be listened on, seperated by whitespace, which the 'daemon
# -l' this replaced could not do, as systemd splits ExecStart on whitespace too
# and only the first would have been taken as the listen. Split on ' ' rather
# than /\s+/ so leading whitespace does not produce an empty listen.
if ( defined( $ENV{'LISTEN_ON'} ) ) {
	my @listen_on = split( ' ', $ENV{'LISTEN_ON'} );
	if (@listen_on) {
		$hypnotoad{'listen'} = \@listen_on;
	}
}

# hypnotoad defaults this to a 'hypnotoad.pid' next to the nergal script, which
# is a bin dir the user it runs as has no business writing to
if ( defined( $ENV{'NERGAL_PID_FILE'} ) ) {
	$hypnotoad{'pid_file'} = $ENV{'NERGAL_PID_FILE'};
}

# A submission blocks its worker until CAPE has been handed the sample, so this
# is how many may be in flight at once. Left to hypnotoad's own default of 4 when
# not set.
if ( defined( $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'} ) ) {
	if ( $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'} =~ /^[1-9]\d*$/ ) {
		$hypnotoad{'workers'} = $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'};
	} else {
		log_drek(
			'err',
			'$ENV{NERGAL_WORKERS} set to "'
				. $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'}
				. '" which does not match /^[1-9]\d*$/ ... leaving it to hypnotoad',
			undef,
			'nergal'
		);
	} ## end else [ if ( $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'} =~ /^[1-9]\d*$/)]
} ## end if ( defined( $ENV{'NERGAL_WORKERS'} ) )

app->config( 'hypnotoad' => \%hypnotoad );

app->start;
